; workload-clock — an SELinux domain for the per-VM clock keeper.
;
; WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS AT ALL
;
; The same finding workload-resolve.cil records, applied before it could be
; made again: a helper under /usr/libexec/workloadctl with no filecon is bin_t,
; systemd's own rules run its service as unconfined_service_t, and NOTHING
; fails. workload-vm-clock is one tick of the guest-clock keeper -- it connects
; to the VM's qemu-guest-agent socket, sends guest-sync and guest-get-time, and
; parses what comes back. What comes back is written by a process inside the
; guest, which is to say by the thing this whole design exists to distrust.
; A parser of guest-supplied bytes on a one-minute timer, running unconfined
; as a matter of course, is the shape both sibling modules were written to end.
;
; MODELLED ON workload-resolve.cil, MINUS EVERYTHING SOCKET ACTIVATION NEEDS
;
; The keeper is a oneshot on a timer, not a socket-activated listener, so none
; of the init_t -> domain socket rules the two siblings carry apply here: init_t
; creates nothing in this domain but the process. What remains is the smallest
; domain in the tree: an entrypoint, the interpreter, the journal, and one
; unix socket connect. Read workload-inspect.cil's header for the surprises
; about the entrypoint and the interpreter; they hold unchanged.
;
; THE GUEST AGENT SOCKET is the whole of what this domain does, and this is
; the only host-side domain that reaches it: workload-inspect.cil carried the
; same three lines for its mint-time clock check until the check moved here,
; which is the point -- the domain that parses what the internet sends and
; the domain that can speak to QEMU's agent channel are now different
; domains. /run/workload-vm/<name>/ga.sock is a socket QEMU created, so
; svirt_t owns the other end, and `sock_file` is a class of its own -- a file
; grant on the directory covers nothing on the socket node, and `connectto`
; is checked on the peer's domain rather than on the file.

(type wlclock_t)
(typeattributeset domain (wlclock_t))
(roletype system_r wlclock_t)

; roletype before the filecon, or semodule rejects the module with
; "Type wlclock_exec_t is invalid for role object_r".
(type wlclock_exec_t)
(roletype object_r wlclock_exec_t)
(typeattributeset file_type (wlclock_exec_t))
(typeattributeset exec_type (wlclock_exec_t))
(typeattributeset entry_type (wlclock_exec_t))
(filecon "/usr/libexec/workloadctl/workload-vm-clock" file (system_u object_r wlclock_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))

(typetransition init_t wlclock_exec_t process wlclock_t)
(allow init_t wlclock_exec_t (file (getattr open read execute map)))
(allow init_t wlclock_t (process (transition siginh)))
(allow wlclock_t wlclock_exec_t (file (entrypoint getattr ioctl open read)))

; The interpreter and the stdlib. See the inspect module for why this is on the
; attribute rather than on bin_t.
(allow wlclock_t bin_t (file (execute map)))

; stdout/stderr to the journal.
(allow wlclock_t init_t (unix_stream_socket (read write)))

; DELIBERATELY NOT GRANTED, on the sibling modules' rule: an entry leaves this
; list only when the domain acquires a real reason for it, never because a
; harvest shows it being attempted. Both are the interpreter starting up and
; each fails silently. Measured 2026-09-20 with `semodule -DB` on two
; enforcing hosts (TSC and hpet) across two full clock_rig runs: these, the
; user-site probe below, and NOTHING else, with every tick reaching the agent
; and the skewed guest repaired in the domain.
;
;   getattr, ioctl on init_t:unix_stream_socket   Python asking whether its
;     stdout is a tty.
;   search on cert_t:dir ("pki")                  libcrypto reading its
;     OPENSSLDIR config, pulled in through hashlib. This domain terminates
;     no TLS and verifies nothing.
;
; Both are DONTAUDIT'D, not merely ungranted. The keeper runs once a minute
; per VM, so an audited residual is three lines a minute per VM forever, and
; on a VM host that volume rotates real denials out of audit.log within a
; day or two. dontaudit keeps them denied and stops the logging; harvest with
; `semodule -DB` as above, which brings them back.
;
; The third startup probe is not attempted at all: site.py stat'ing
; $HOME/.local/lib/pythonX.Y/site-packages, a search on container_file_t:dir
; ("workloads") because the unit sets User= to the workload user and $HOME is
; that user's home under /var/lib/workloads. The keeper's `-s` shebang turns
; the user site off, so there is no probe to deny or to hide.
(dontaudit wlclock_t init_t (unix_stream_socket (getattr ioctl)))
(dontaudit wlclock_t cert_t (dir (search)))

; The guest agent socket: search the VM's runtime directory, write the socket
; node, connect to QEMU's end of it. `search` only on the directory -- the
; keeper creates nothing there and reads no document from it, which is the
; difference from both siblings' `add_name remove_name write`.
(allow wlclock_t qemu_var_run_t (dir (search)))
(allow wlclock_t qemu_var_run_t (sock_file (getattr write)))
(allow wlclock_t svirt_t (unix_stream_socket (connectto)))
(allow wlclock_t self (unix_stream_socket (connect create getattr read shutdown write)))
